WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.899
INTRO:
You may know Peter Thiel as one of the cofounders

00:00:02.899 --> 00:00:04.160
of PayPal.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:09.490
He masterfully negotiated an acquisition deal
with eBay worth $1.5 billion that made the

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:11.009
PayPal team quite wealthy.

00:00:11.009 --> 00:00:16.820
Peter’s take from the acquisition was a
solid $60 million, but this is not even 1%

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.960
of Peter’s current net worth of $9.18 billion.

00:00:19.960 --> 00:00:25.960
You see, while Peter is most famous for his
involvement with PayPal, PayPal was not even

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:30.100
close to being his most successful investment
or business endeavor.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.149
Aside from being a business genius, Peter
is no stranger to drama and rivalry.

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:38.820
He’s had this love-hate relationship with
Elon for nearly 20 years at this point, and

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:45.180
in the past, he’s waited years to take revenge
on those who wronged him.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.350
So, here’s the spicy rise of Peter Thiel.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:55.480
PETER THIEL:
Taking a look back, Peter was born on October

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.980
11, 1967, in Frankfurt Germany.

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:03.800
At the time, Germany was split up into east
and west Germany, and luckily for Peter, Frankfurt

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:05.630
was located on the west side.

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:09.740
I doubt Peter remembers very much from his
early days in Germany though as his parents

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:13.110
would move to Cleveland, Ohio when he was
just 1 year old.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:17.580
In the US, Peter’s father was able to score
a solid job as a chemical engineer, but while

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:21.689
this job provided the family with financial
stability, it didn’t provide them with much

00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:22.689
life stability.

00:01:22.689 --> 00:01:27.850
The job required the family to be constantly
moving around not just the US but the entire

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:28.850
world.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:32.240
By the time Peter finished elementary school,
he had switched schools 7 times, and one of

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.619
these schools was actually located in modern-day
Namibia.

00:01:34.619 --> 00:01:39.810
Fortunately, the family was finally able to
settle down in Foster City, California around

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.640
the time Peter hit middle school.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:45.439
Throughout his school days, Peter had two
main passions which were reading sci-fi books

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:47.060
and playing chess.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.090
Peter says that he’s read the Lord Of The
Rings over 10 times, and 6 of his companies

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:54.010
are actually named after references to the
book and the author.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:57.810
As for chess, Peter started playing the game
when he was 6 and he would become one of the

00:01:57.810 --> 00:01:59.259
strongest junior competitors.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:03.329
In fact, he was so good that he was awarded
the title of Life Master.

00:02:03.329 --> 00:02:05.810
Chess wasn’t Peter’s only strong suit
though.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:10.340
He was particularly talented in mathematics
and he would graduate as valedictorian.

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.990
After high school, Peter would attend Stanford
where he majored in philosophy, and though

00:02:13.990 --> 00:02:18.130
he enjoyed the content, he wasn’t a fan
of the liberal biases embedded within the

00:02:18.130 --> 00:02:19.130
education.

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:22.940
This was one of Peter’s first entanglements
with controversy as he would turn around and

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.170
cofound The Stanford Review which was a conservative-libertarian
newspaper.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.980
Peter served as the editor-in-chief up until
graduation, but that wasn’t the end of his

00:02:30.980 --> 00:02:31.980
support.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:35.471
He still donates to the newspaper to this
day and he commonly hires students from the

00:02:35.471 --> 00:02:36.471
newspaper.

00:02:36.471 --> 00:02:40.750
Anyway, after his undergraduate days, Peter
would turn around and attend Stanford once

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:42.660
again to get his Juris Doctor degree.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.710
And yes, this means that Peter was formally
trained to be a lawyer.

00:02:46.710 --> 00:02:51.180
After graduation in 1992, he started clerking
for a judge in the US appeals court, and he

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.230
would eventually work up to being a securities
lawyer in New York.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.310
But Peter would end up quitting this job and
basically law as a whole just 7 months later

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:01.900
stating that he found little transcendental
value in this work.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.710
Peter bounced around a little after leaving
law, shortly working as a derivatives trader

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:07.380
and as a speechwriter.

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:11.730
But in 1996, he decided to return back to
California and look for a more meaningful

00:03:11.730 --> 00:03:12.730
job.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:18.180
He never found this perfect job, but he would
find a perfect career.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.900
PAYPAL:
Peter didn’t just jump straight into PayPal;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:24.820
in fact, he wasn’t even looking to start
his own business.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.590
He was, however, determined to get involved
in an internet company as he saw the dot-com

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.980
boom unfold, so he became a venture capitalist.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:36.790
In 1996, Peter started Thiel Capital Management
and funded it using money from friends, family,

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:37.960
and his personal savings.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:41.920
Peter’s first investment was $100,000 into
a man named Luke Nosek.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.690
Luke had the idea of creating a web-based
calendar, but this idea didn’t quite pan

00:03:46.690 --> 00:03:47.690
out.

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.350
Peter’s second investment was also in Luke
Nosek.

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:55.010
But this time, Max Levchin was also involved
and the idea was to create a digital cryptography

00:03:55.010 --> 00:03:56.980
company called Confinity.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:01.150
The company originally sold security software
for handheld devices, but they found little

00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:02.750
success in this area.

00:04:02.750 --> 00:04:06.480
So, Peter decided to shift the company into
fintech with the launch of something called

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:07.480
PayPal.

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:12.270
Today, PayPal has basically become ubiquitous
with online payments, but this wasn’t Peter’s

00:04:12.270 --> 00:04:13.270
intention.

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:17.780
You see, Peter’s goal with PayPal was much
more philosophical and intellectual.

00:04:17.780 --> 00:04:22.470
Peter wanted to create a sort of decentralized
rebellion against inflation similar to Bitcoin.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:26.260
Peter hated that the government was inflating
away currency and he thought that the solution

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:30.260
was creating a digital exchange that allowed
people to easily switch between currencies.

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:34.530
This way, PayPal users could basically boycott
certain currencies and force governments to

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:35.530
respond.

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:40.030
In 1999, Peter said quote, “It will be nearly
impossible for corrupt governments to steal

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.870
wealth from their people through their old
means because if they try the people will

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:48.650
switch to dollars or Pounds or Yen, in effect
dumping the worthless local currency for something

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:49.790
more secure.”

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:54.190
It looks to me like Peter was begging to be
on the government’s watchlist, but you know,

00:04:54.190 --> 00:04:56.240
this was just Peter being Peter.

00:04:56.240 --> 00:05:01.140
Unfortunately, though, the general public
didn’t respond as Peter expected and PayPal

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:03.830
ended up as just a popular mode of payment.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.660
Despite their popularity, running the business
wasn’t easy given how much cash they were

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:08.660
bleeding.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:13.340
So, in the early 2000s, PayPal would merge
with a competitor called X.com and this is

00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:15.260
where Elon would get involved.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:19.280
The merger helped secure PayPal’s market
share, but their financial situation didn’t

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:20.420
get any better.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:24.930
So, the company was forced to go public and
they would soon be sold to eBay.

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:29.990
While this made Peter quite wealthy, this
was just the beginning.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:34.050
SERIAL ENTREPRENEUR:
One of the first things Peter did after selling

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.520
PayPal was starting Clarium Capital.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.890
Clarium was a macro hedge fund focussed on
trading currencies, interest rates, commodities,

00:05:40.890 --> 00:05:41.890
and equities.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:45.640
And Peter based the firm’s investment thesis
around the peak-oil theory which states that

00:05:45.640 --> 00:05:49.080
the world is running out of oil and there
are no easy alternatives.

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:53.930
This strategy proved to be extremely successful
for Peter as Clarium posted 40% year after

00:05:53.930 --> 00:05:54.930
40% year.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:59.930
He grew the fund from just $10 million from
himself in 2002 to $7 billion in 2008.

00:05:59.930 --> 00:06:05.360
But, by 2011, most investors left the fund,
not because Clarium got crushed by the financial

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:08.759
crisis but because Peter missed out on buying
the dip.

00:06:08.759 --> 00:06:13.340
This was quite unfortunate, but by this point,
Peter’s initial $10 million had grown to

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:17.530
$230 million, so I think it worked out just
fine.

00:06:17.530 --> 00:06:22.310
Aside from starting Clarium, Peter also founded
Palantir in May of 2003.

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:25.880
Palantir is a data analytics company that
helps the government track down criminals,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.510
monitor supply chains, and more recently predict
covid outbreaks.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:33.840
Palantir has always been a popular investment
within the tech world, but since their IPO

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:38.270
in 2020, Palantir has become a popular choice
amongst retail investors as well.

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:43.650
The company saw valuations as high as $80
billion following their IPO, but they have

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.580
since settled down at a more modest $38 billion.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:51.440
Around the same time as starting Palantir,
Peter became an angel investor in Facebook.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.770
In 2004, he invested $500,000 in exchange
for 10.2% of the company.

00:06:56.770 --> 00:07:00.530
This gave him a spot on the board and Peter
actually helped Facebook with future funding

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:01.530
rounds.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:05.430
But, ever since Facebook went public, Peter
has been slowly selling off his stake.

00:07:05.430 --> 00:07:09.560
He still has a few thousand shares of Facebook
left but compared to his 16.8 million shares

00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:12.420
when Facebook went public, this is nothing.

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.310
Though Peter gave up most of his shares, he
did cash out over a billion dollars securing

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:18.680
a casual 2000x return.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.520
And did I mention, Facebook was just one of
his star performers.

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:26.830
In 2005, Peter created something called the
Founders Fund which was focused on capitalizing

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:28.450
on early-stage companies.

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:31.410
And their list of insane investments literally
goes on forever.

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:37.680
We have Airbnb, Slide.com, LinkedIn, Friendster,
RapLeaf, Geni.com, Yammer, Yelp Inc., Spotify,

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.440
Powerset, Practice Fusion, MetaMed, Vator,
SpaceX, IronPort, Votizen, Big Think, CapLinked,

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:49.050
Quora, Nanotronics Imaging, Rypple, TransferWise,
Stripe, Block.one, and AltSchool.

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:53.210
Peter also purchased $15 to $20 million worth
of bitcoin in mid-2017.

00:07:53.210 --> 00:07:57.800
And he gave Vitalik Buterin $100,000 to drop
out of college and found Ethereum.

00:07:57.800 --> 00:08:03.410
Like I said, Peter is literally involved in
everything, and we still have more to cover.

00:08:03.410 --> 00:08:07.740
In 2012, Peter founded Valar Ventures which
is focused on investing in startups outside

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:09.180
of Silicon Valley.

00:08:09.180 --> 00:08:12.940
That same year, he also founded Mithril Capital
which is focused on investing in mid-stage

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:15.050
companies that are ready to scale.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:19.880
On top of all of this, Peter became a part-time
partner at Y Combinator in March of 2015.

00:08:19.880 --> 00:08:25.040
However, this only lasted two years as Peter
quickly formed some beef with the other leaders,

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:28.320
and that brings us into Peter’s beef with
everyone.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:32.839
BEEF:
We’ve already covered Peter’s beef with

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:37.090
Stanford’s super liberal views and his hatred
for inflation, but this was just scratching

00:08:37.090 --> 00:08:38.320
the surface.

00:08:38.320 --> 00:08:42.180
Peter has a tendency of hating institutions
in general and this makes sense given that

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:43.490
he’s a libertarian.

00:08:43.490 --> 00:08:47.250
One of Peter’s most controversial views
is his loathing of higher education.

00:08:47.250 --> 00:08:51.810
Of course, Peter isn’t against education
or learning by any means, but he strongly

00:08:51.810 --> 00:08:55.800
believes that modern universities are overpriced
relics that are holding back real innovation.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.300
He thinks that people should skip college,
that higher education has brainwashed Silicon

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:04.940
Valley, that higher education is the biggest
bubble in the world, and that brilliant kids

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.480
don’t need college.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.100
One of his efforts in fighting college has
been the Thiel Fellowship which offers students

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:13.140
$100,000 to drop out of school and start a
business instead.

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.950
Ethereum was actually a product of one of
these fellowships.

00:09:15.950 --> 00:09:19.110
Peter’s beef isn’t just limited to institutions
either.

00:09:19.110 --> 00:09:23.800
He has plenty of beef with people as well
with one of his notable beefs being with Elon

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:24.800
Musk.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:29.300
As we touched on before, PayPal merged with
X.com in the early 2000s, and Elon would eventually

00:09:29.300 --> 00:09:31.460
become the CEO of the joint company.

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:35.930
But, Elon had very different views about the
future of the company and branding.

00:09:35.930 --> 00:09:39.950
Peter and the rest of the board didn’t really
agree with this vision, so they would fire

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:41.460
Elon while he was on vacation.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:46.470
Elon obviously felt that this was a backstab
move from Peter, and this is where the beef

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:47.470
really started.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:51.220
Peter didn’t mean for this to be personal
and both of them have tried to make amends.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:55.740
For example, Peter would invest $20 million
in SpaceX in 2008 and help save the company

00:09:55.740 --> 00:09:56.850
from bankruptcy.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:10:01.910
Also, I’m sure they both have immense respect
for each other’s skills and capabilities.

00:10:01.910 --> 00:10:05.990
But with that being said, they still trash
talk each other all the time.

00:10:05.990 --> 00:10:10.000
Peter has called Elon a fraud and a braggart
and he thinks that Elon isn’t a good role

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.000
model.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.490
Meanwhile, Elon thinks that Peter is a sociopath.

00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:18.400
Clearly, Peter and Elon have a love-hate relationship,
but not all of Peter’s relationships are

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:19.840
so benign.

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:24.100
Some of his relationships are just hate-hate
relationships, and the best example of this

00:10:24.100 --> 00:10:25.490
is the Gawker lawsuit.

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:31.110
In 2016, Terry Bollea filed a lawsuit against
Gawker Media for invasion of privacy and emotional

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:32.110
distress.

00:10:32.110 --> 00:10:36.720
Peter had nothing to do with the lawsuit itself,
but he decided to fund Terry with $10 million

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.790
to fight against Gawker Media, and this helped
Terry win a $140 million lawsuit against Gawker

00:10:41.790 --> 00:10:43.770
which forced the media company to shut down.

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:49.210
Now, Terry was in the right so Peter was funding
justice, but this was not the main reason

00:10:49.210 --> 00:10:51.550
that Peter decided to help Terry.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:55.570
You see, Peter had his own beef with Gawker
from nearly a decade ago.

00:10:55.570 --> 00:11:00.640
In 2007, Gawker media posted an article titled,
Peter Thiel is totally gay, people.

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.610
As you would guess, Peter was not a big fan
of this article and he made it his personal

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.360
mission to take down the company.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:11.090
Peter says that taking down Gawker was less
about revenge and more about specific deterrence,

00:11:11.090 --> 00:11:13.180
but I’ll let you be the judge of that.

00:11:13.180 --> 00:11:18.800
Anyway, while some of Peter’s beef is personal
and a little childish, most of it is intellectually

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:19.800
fueled.

00:11:19.800 --> 00:11:24.650
After all, Peter basically envisioned bitcoin
22 years ago, so I think it’s just a matter

00:11:24.650 --> 00:11:27.450
of time until his other assertions also become
a reality.

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:31.660
Now, this doesn’t mean you should just go
ahead and drop out of college or anything.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.550
But I think it’s definitely a good idea
to keep Peter’s arguments in the back of

00:11:34.550 --> 00:11:36.990
your head as you navigate through life.

00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:38.290
But that’s just what I think.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:42.220
Do you think Peter is a genius visionary or
just a rogue troublemaker?

00:11:42.220 --> 00:11:43.580
Comment that down below.

00:11:43.580 --> 00:11:47.140
Also, drop a like if you’ll be keeping Peter’s
remarks in mind.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:50.730
And of course, consider joining our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:56.030
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

